<mujoco model="xarm6_pusht_icra_sign">
  <!-- Push a letter into the gap in a sign. Seven fixed letters stand in a
       row spelling "ICRA 2026" with the C's own slot left empty; the goal
       is that slot, so a solved run completes the word.

       IsaacGym conf/task/sim_task05.yaml, which spells "IROS 2026" and
       pushes its S. Same task, different word: the row spacing, glyph
       sizes, goal orientation and start pose are the source's; only the
       letters change.

       This is the one scene whose pushed object is not the T, so it does
       not share tee.xml -- it declares its own table, camera, object and
       goal below. The arm, physics and room still come from common.xml.

       Its xy re-origin also differs: the pushed letter starts at
       (0.7, -0.40) in IsaacGym (sim_task05.yaml's object-side
       `initial_actor_positions`; conf/actors/S.yaml itself leaves init_pos
       commented out because that task is normally launched with a
       `scenario_id` from examples/xarm6_stick_push/scenario_init_poses.yaml
       that overwrites it). So the shift here is (-0.7, +0.40), not the
       T-block scenes' (-0.7, +0.45).

       WHY THE C IS THE PUSHED LETTER. In "IROS" the source pushes the S,
       the only glyph in that word with no enclosed counter. "ICRA" has two
       such glyphs, I and C, and C is the closer analogue of S: an open
       curve with one deep concavity, where I is a plain bar. It also
       matters mechanically -- a footprint is a single `Polygon`, which
       cannot express a hole, so pushing the R or the A would force the
       planner to treat that letter's counter as solid while the simulator
       has a hole there. tests/test_scenes.py could not catch that
       divergence, since it only checks the outline both ways.

       LETTERS ARE BOUNDING BOXES, NOT MESHES. The seven obstacle glyphs are
       `fixed: True`, so only their footprint matters, and each is the
       axis-aligned bounding box of its own `*_centered.ply` (scale 0.001)
       from the source assets. That keeps the simulated geometry and the
       planner's obstacle field (oim/utils/scenes.py) exactly equal, and
       avoids MJX convex-mesh collision entirely. The row still reads as a
       sign: glyphs are 0.15 m apart and ~0.07 m wide, so the gaps survive.

       The pushed C is a three-box block letter (see `c_shape_footprint` in
       oim/objects/planar_pushing.py, which builds the matching outline).
       That IS a simplification -- the source glyphs are smoothly curved --
       but it keeps the bounding box, the 0.025 m thickness, the stroke
       width and the single concavity, which are the properties that decide
       how the letter can be pushed. -->
  <include file="common.xml"/>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- Same IsaacGym "table" actor as the T-block scenes, shifted by this
         scene's own (-0.7, +0.40). Covers x in [-0.4, 1.0], y in
         [-0.85, 1.65]; the letter row spans y in [-0.20, 1.05], so it stays
         well inside. -->
    <geom name="table" type="box" size="0.70 1.25 0.07" pos="0.3 0.40 -0.07"
      rgba="0.95 0.95 0.95 1"/>

    <!-- Same framing as the T-block scenes' camera (azimuth=180,
         elevation=-25) but pulled back to distance 2.1 and centred on this
         scene's own workspace, lookat (-0.05, 0.42, 0.15): the letter row
         is 1.25 m long, far wider than that layout, and at 1.7 the "I" and
         the "6" fell outside the frame. -->
    <camera name="front" pos="1.8532 0.4200 1.0375"
      xyaxes="0.0000 1.0000 -0.0000 -0.4226 0.0000 0.9063"/>

    <!-- Mirrors the C's geoms exactly, at the goal pose: the empty slot in
         the row, second from the top, at (0.9, 0.45) in IsaacGym's
         coordinates -> (0.2, 0.85) here. Orientation is the source's own
         letter quat, init_ori [0, 0, 0.7071, -0.7071] (xyzw) = -90 degrees
         about z, which as an MJCF wxyz quat is (0.70711, 0, 0, -0.70711).
         The C spawns unrotated, so this task needs a quarter turn as well
         as a translation. -->
    <body name="goal" mocap="true" pos="0.2 0.85 0.0125" quat="0.70711 0 0 -0.70711">
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_spine" type="box" size="0.0100 0.0515 0.0125" pos="-0.025 0.0 0.0"/>
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_top_bar" type="box" size="0.0350 0.0100 0.0125" pos="0.0 0.0415 0.0"/>
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_bot_bar" type="box" size="0.0350 0.0100 0.0125" pos="0.0 -0.0415 0.0"/>
    </body>

    <!-- The pushed C. The body name "block" and the joint names T_x/T_y/T_z
         are what oim/tasks/pusht.py looks up, so they are the same in every
         PushT scene regardless of the object's shape. Same frictionloss
         tuning as tee.xml (mu*m*g = 7.848, c*r*mu*m*g = 0.47088) and the
         same 2.0 kg total, split across the three geoms in proportion to
         their area so the mass is distributed like the letter, not lumped.

         0.070 x 0.103 m overall with a 0.020 m stroke. The height and
         thickness are the source glyphs' own (every letter in
         assets/urdf/alphabets measures 100-103 mm tall and 25 mm thick);
         the width sits between the measured O (73.6 mm) and 0 (67.9 mm),
         the two roundest glyphs in that font, since there is no C mesh to
         measure. -->
    <body name="block" pos="0.0 0.0 0.0125">
      <joint name="T_x" type="slide" axis="1 0 0" frictionloss="7.848" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_y" type="slide" axis="0 1 0" frictionloss="7.848" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_z" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" frictionloss="0.47088" damping="0.0"/>
      <geom name="c_spine" type="box" size="0.0100 0.0515 0.0125" pos="-0.025 0.0 0.0" mass="0.848" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
      <geom name="c_top_bar" type="box" size="0.0350 0.0100 0.0125" pos="0.0 0.0415 0.0" mass="0.576" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
      <geom name="c_bot_bar" type="box" size="0.0350 0.0100 0.0125" pos="0.0 -0.0415 0.0" mass="0.576" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
    </body>

    <!-- "ICRA 2026" minus its C, one static geom per glyph. Every slot
         shares init_pos x = 0.9 (-> 0.2 here) and init_ori
         [0, 0, 0.7071, -0.7071] (xyzw) = -90 degrees about z; `euler` below
         is in degrees, per <compiler angle="degree"> in ../xarm6/xarm6.xml.
         The y values are IsaacGym's own row: 0.6, 0.45, 0.3, 0.15 for the
         four letters, then -0.1, -0.25, -0.4, -0.55 for the year, all
         shifted by +0.40. The gap at y = 0.85 is where the C goes.

         `size` is half of each glyph's own bounding box (mm, scale 0.001):
         I 24.3 x 100.0, R 71.3 x 100.0, 2 66.8 x 101.5, 0 67.9 x 103.0,
         6 65.9 x 103.0. The A reuses R's box -- the source assets have no A
         mesh, and R is the same font at the same cap height. -->
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="letter_I" type="box"
      size="0.01216 0.05000 0.0125" pos="0.2 1.00 0.0125" euler="0 0 -90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="letter_R" type="box"
      size="0.03563 0.05000 0.0125" pos="0.2 0.70 0.0125" euler="0 0 -90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="letter_A" type="box"
      size="0.03563 0.05000 0.0125" pos="0.2 0.55 0.0125" euler="0 0 -90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_2" type="box"
      size="0.03338 0.05076 0.0125" pos="0.2 0.30 0.0125" euler="0 0 -90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_0" type="box"
      size="0.03393 0.05152 0.0125" pos="0.2 0.15 0.0125" euler="0 0 -90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_2b" type="box"
      size="0.03338 0.05076 0.0125" pos="0.2 0.00 0.0125" euler="0 0 -90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_6" type="box"
      size="0.03295 0.05152 0.0125" pos="0.2 -0.15 0.0125" euler="0 0 -90"/>
  </worldbody>

  <sensor>
    <framepos name="position" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
    <framequat name="orientation" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
  </sensor>

  <statistic center="-0.05 0.42 0" extent="0.9"/>
</mujoco>
